  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "One run in nine strikeouts is not an offensive performance. It's a warning.",
    "what_this_means": "They never led. Angel Stadium took that from them in the second inning, and nine empty at-bats afterward made clear the bats were somewhere else. This was a day when the pitching held. Civale's 5.0 innings with four earned runs could have been a start you build around. But the offense didn't meet it. Eight runners left on base across eight innings. One converted. The bullpen bridge worked (Alvarado and Suarez combined for three clean) but by the time it mattered, there was no game left to finish. This is what happens when a team that already sits fourth in the division, two back, leaves that many runners and scores that little. The math doesn't get easier.",
    "what_to_watch": "LAD arrive tonight with Eric Lauer, whose 4.87 ERA invites the Athletics to put early pressure on him. If the bats show up. Gage Jump takes the mound in the sharpest stretch of his season, carrying a 1.17 ERA over four starts. This is the kind of matchup the lineup needs to prove yesterday was an outlier, not a preview.",
